KNOWNBUG
justice_polarity1.btor2

^\[justice3\] : REFUTED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
BTOR2 justice/fair nodes are liveness (generalized-Buchi) violation
patterns, analogous to `bad`: finding a reachable path along which the
justice condition holds infinitely often means the property is
VIOLATED (should be REFUTED), exactly as reaching a `bad` state means a
safety property is violated. Unlike `bad` (correctly negated to
G(!bad) in btor_ebmc_language.cpp), `justice` is instead lowered
directly as G(F(condition)) and checked with the ordinary *universal*
engine -- the polarity is inverted. Here the justice condition is the
constant 1, so a fair witness trivially exists on every path, yet the
tool reports PROVED -- in fact recognized as an outright tautology, no
solver call needed -- instead of REFUTED.
